[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

400
00:26:02,086 --> 00:26:07,030
[SPEAKER_01]: From that necessary basis, even for such attempts of such escape.

401
00:26:33,580 --> 00:26:43,063
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why are... why is sensuality dangerous?

402
00:26:43,083 --> 00:26:44,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is sensuality dangerous?

403
00:26:44,644 --> 00:26:46,105
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we... wasn't that where we were?

404
00:26:46,225 --> 00:26:47,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Wasn't that your question earlier?

405
00:26:48,866 --> 00:26:49,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe.

406
00:26:52,587 --> 00:26:54,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is the gratification dangerous?

407
00:26:57,489 --> 00:26:58,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's what you asked.

408
00:26:58,829 --> 00:27:00,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is the gratification dangerous?

409
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:04,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the whole thing is in sensuality.

410
00:27:05,661 --> 00:27:08,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratification, danger and escape, it's off the central domain.

411
00:27:09,722 --> 00:27:14,203
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is the act of regarding pain as pleasant, dangerous?

412
00:27:14,383 --> 00:27:19,484
[SPEAKER_00]: The act of regarding pain as pleasure.

413
00:27:20,684 --> 00:27:25,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the act of feeling pain as pleasure, as in acting out of it.

414
00:27:26,146 --> 00:27:28,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not your act to feel it like this, it's your mistake.

415
00:27:28,806 --> 00:27:31,007
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is acting out of that?

416
00:27:32,181 --> 00:27:32,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Dangerous.

417
00:27:32,681 --> 00:27:32,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

418
00:27:32,901 --> 00:27:39,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a sensually enchanting object, prospect of it nearby.

419
00:27:40,045 --> 00:27:41,366
[SPEAKER_01]: You can access it if you want.

420
00:27:42,126 --> 00:27:49,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that experience as a whole, that feeling, underlying feeling of that prospect that you are now in, is unpleasant.

421
00:27:50,511 --> 00:27:53,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the pleasure that's wanted is not here.

422
00:27:54,173 --> 00:28:00,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And why you want that pleasure is not because the thing is pleasant, but because the arising of the prospect is painful.

423
00:28:01,692 --> 00:28:03,332
[SPEAKER_00]: That determines how much you want pleasure.

424
00:28:05,573 --> 00:28:13,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as we described in the other talks, if the pleasure were truly contained in the objects, you wouldn't be able to know it before you're in touch with the objects.

425
00:28:14,314 --> 00:28:18,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly would not be able to not feel pleasure when you're in touch with the objects, which also happens.

426
00:28:18,815 --> 00:28:20,975
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure arises with the prospect of having that thing.

427
00:28:22,236 --> 00:28:25,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, because the prospect of having the thing is painful.

428
00:28:25,616 --> 00:28:28,937
[SPEAKER_01]: So, following that... Because I've not got it, and I want it.

429
00:28:29,197 --> 00:28:30,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Following that prospect,

430
00:28:31,457 --> 00:28:32,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that dangerous?

431
00:28:34,981 --> 00:28:39,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're reinforcing this wrong direction.

432
00:28:39,946 --> 00:28:42,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is the wrong direction wrong?

433
00:28:44,530 --> 00:28:48,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you aren't seeing what's painful as painful.

434
00:28:49,115 --> 00:28:52,558
[SPEAKER_00]: You aren't seeing... Why is that risky?

435
00:28:54,020 --> 00:28:54,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Danger.

436
00:28:54,680 --> 00:28:54,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Risk.

437
00:28:56,002 --> 00:29:01,565
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, you know, knowing gratification, the danger became apparent, the risk became apparent, as it should be said.

438
00:29:02,285 --> 00:29:03,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is it risky?

439
00:29:03,466 --> 00:29:17,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... Because, actually, you have no control over the arising of pleasure.

440
00:29:18,395 --> 00:29:18,935
[SPEAKER_01]: What pleasure?

441
00:29:19,396 --> 00:29:19,996
[SPEAKER_01]: This pain.

442
00:29:21,197 --> 00:29:23,118
[SPEAKER_00]: This pleasure.

443
00:29:23,599 --> 00:29:35,207
[SPEAKER_00]: This possibility of the diminishing of the pain may not... You can't make that happen, no matter what you do.

444
00:29:36,568 --> 00:29:38,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, it is a gratification, though.

445
00:29:39,129 --> 00:29:41,271
[SPEAKER_01]: It does gratify to some extent.

446
00:29:41,891 --> 00:29:46,715
[SPEAKER_01]: The burning embers do help Leper with his skin disease.

447
00:29:48,419 --> 00:29:50,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no risk there, then.

448
00:29:52,260 --> 00:29:53,600
[SPEAKER_01]: If that would be the sole criteria.

449
00:29:53,620 --> 00:29:55,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is a degree of gratification.

450
00:29:55,681 --> 00:29:56,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that can't be the risk.

451
00:29:56,981 --> 00:29:57,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is then?

452
00:29:57,901 --> 00:30:00,742
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the risk in that gratification that you can get?

453
00:30:02,563 --> 00:30:07,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, from the point of view of pain that just pressured me.

454
00:30:08,263 --> 00:30:10,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Less pain is a form of escape.

455
00:30:10,684 --> 00:30:11,145
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pleasure.

456
00:30:11,545 --> 00:30:12,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

457
00:30:12,585 --> 00:30:13,386
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more agreeable.

458
00:30:13,426 --> 00:30:16,047
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more desirable, because it's pleasant.

459
00:30:16,628 --> 00:30:22,291
[SPEAKER_01]: But, giving in to that, contains an inherent danger, a risk.

460
00:30:22,491 --> 00:30:23,231
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the risk?

461
00:30:23,331 --> 00:30:24,872
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to say, because it's kind of...

462
00:30:27,215 --> 00:30:28,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep reinforcing.

463
00:30:28,276 --> 00:30:30,899
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to reinforce this particular direction.

464
00:30:31,659 --> 00:30:32,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

465
00:30:32,100 --> 00:30:34,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That happens as a result of it.

466
00:30:34,422 --> 00:30:35,803
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the apparent risk.

467
00:30:36,043 --> 00:30:38,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, this result will happen after I do this.

468
00:30:38,826 --> 00:30:38,946
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

469
00:30:38,966 --> 00:30:40,127
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a risk in it.

470
00:30:40,368 --> 00:30:42,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Imply risk before you do it.

471
00:30:42,289 --> 00:30:43,150
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what risk is.

472
00:30:43,190 --> 00:30:44,972
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what means something's risky.

473
00:30:45,512 --> 00:30:46,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is risky.

474
00:30:46,774 --> 00:30:49,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing risk, you don't need to wait for the outcome to see the risk.

475
00:30:49,937 --> 00:30:51,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you don't know the risk before you do it.

476
00:30:53,486 --> 00:30:58,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So, directly, before you do it, at the prospect of doing it, you discern that there is a risk in doing it.

477
00:31:00,128 --> 00:31:00,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

478
00:31:01,029 --> 00:31:01,989
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the risky bit?

479
00:31:02,069 --> 00:31:10,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha says it's... that these things are impermanent, unownable, subject to change.

480
00:31:11,953 --> 00:31:12,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Which things?

481
00:31:12,393 --> 00:31:12,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings?

482
00:31:13,353 --> 00:31:13,993
[SPEAKER_00]: No, eh...

483
00:31:18,315 --> 00:31:18,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

484
00:31:20,275 --> 00:31:21,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's say on the level of feelings.

485
00:31:21,456 --> 00:31:21,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

486
00:31:22,845 --> 00:31:32,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's, again, if you don't feel unpleasant when the prospect of desirable object comes your way, you wouldn't be pressured to act towards it.

487
00:31:32,767 --> 00:31:33,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

488
00:31:33,967 --> 00:31:36,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's not leave that necessary basis out.

489
00:31:37,728 --> 00:31:38,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, go on.

490
00:31:39,708 --> 00:31:40,988
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impermanent, unownable.

491
00:31:41,648 --> 00:31:42,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

492
00:31:47,169 --> 00:31:49,230
[SPEAKER_00]: This, eh...

493
00:31:50,399 --> 00:31:56,322
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility of pleasure, the diminishing of...

494
00:31:56,562 --> 00:32:14,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, which is seen as painful, is completely out of my hands and could change at any minute.

495
00:32:15,590 --> 00:32:15,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one?

496
00:32:16,390 --> 00:32:17,371
[SPEAKER_01]: What can change?

497
00:32:18,011 --> 00:32:19,712
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure, the pain,

498
00:32:22,232 --> 00:32:23,913
[SPEAKER_00]: But what if you change it the way you want to change?

499
00:32:25,114 --> 00:32:26,395
[SPEAKER_00]: You get your desires gratified?

500
00:32:26,415 --> 00:32:27,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that could happen.

501
00:32:27,756 --> 00:32:28,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it could go the other way.

502
00:32:29,217 --> 00:32:29,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that risky?

503
00:32:30,658 --> 00:32:31,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it could go the other way.

504
00:32:31,718 --> 00:32:32,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

505
00:32:33,219 --> 00:32:34,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's always a gamble.

506
00:32:34,440 --> 00:32:34,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

507
00:32:35,721 --> 00:32:39,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And now... but, you know, 50-50 gambles, you have a good chance of winning.

508
00:32:40,424 --> 00:32:41,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but how?

509
00:32:43,626 --> 00:32:49,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, so, actually, so, so, because you're not in any control over the feelings that arise,

510
00:32:51,526 --> 00:32:54,669
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm reaching out for some pleasure and it could end up in more pain.

511
00:32:56,050 --> 00:32:56,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

512
00:32:56,791 --> 00:32:59,133
[SPEAKER_01]: That's always a risk of any project end of the world.

513
00:33:00,053 --> 00:33:05,017
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, that's not a fundamental risk that would make you not go that way at all.

514
00:33:05,438 --> 00:33:07,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, from that point of view, it's 50-50.

515
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:08,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

516
00:33:08,941 --> 00:33:14,085
[SPEAKER_01]: But when the Buddha says that danger becomes apparent, means you cannot go back to sensuality ever again.

517
00:33:14,125 --> 00:33:16,447
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he gives a simile, like a stone broken in half,

518
00:33:17,055 --> 00:33:18,235
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot be joined together.

519
00:33:18,575 --> 00:33:20,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a vomited food, they cannot be desirable.

520
00:33:21,016 --> 00:33:23,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a dried-up leaf, they cannot become green again.

521
00:33:23,597 --> 00:33:29,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And that'll be because you now see that whether...

522
00:33:29,339 --> 00:33:30,579
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is deeper.

523
00:33:30,619 --> 00:33:30,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

524
00:33:30,919 --> 00:33:38,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever is... whatever feeling arises, whether it's painful or pleasant, that's something that you were subjected to.

525
00:33:38,542 --> 00:33:42,323
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, in itself, is unpleasant.

526
00:33:44,011 --> 00:33:48,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's on the level of you being pressured by the arisen prospect of sensuality, or something like that.

527
00:33:49,315 --> 00:33:54,719
[SPEAKER_00]: But even being... even being hit by pleasure is something that you were hit by.

528
00:33:54,739 --> 00:33:56,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what I'm saying.

529
00:33:56,180 --> 00:33:58,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Hit by the prospect of sensuality, which is regarded as pleasant.

530
00:33:58,962 --> 00:33:59,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

531
00:34:00,323 --> 00:34:01,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's absolutely true.

532
00:34:02,585 --> 00:34:11,432
[SPEAKER_01]: So, these things, they kind of change in a way of like, oh, look, now I have an opportunity for this great desire, for gratification of it.

533
00:34:14,110 --> 00:34:17,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That's on... oh, that's on the... like that... that fundamental... that still endures.

534
00:34:18,112 --> 00:34:19,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're affected by that.

535
00:34:19,213 --> 00:34:23,375
[SPEAKER_01]: And while you're affected by that desire, that's the only way you'll be acting to fulfill the desire.

536
00:34:24,116 --> 00:34:30,299
[SPEAKER_01]: If the desire were not unpleasant in its nature, you wouldn't be pressured to do anything.

537
00:34:30,319 --> 00:34:36,283
[SPEAKER_01]: And pressuring to do anything, or following the pressure to do anything, is where gratification is.

538
00:34:36,303 --> 00:34:39,385
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, when you understand that, you understand the danger.

539
00:34:40,165 --> 00:34:41,606
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger becomes apparent.

540
00:34:42,902 --> 00:34:45,784
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger of your act, not of what you are subjected to.

541
00:34:45,804 --> 00:34:48,045
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger of responding to the fact that you are pressured.

542
00:34:48,506 --> 00:34:48,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

543
00:34:50,467 --> 00:34:52,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it dangerous to do that?

544
00:34:54,970 --> 00:35:03,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the fact that you are pressured by, say, let's stick to the example, a central object that offers itself, you can have it.

545
00:35:04,336 --> 00:35:05,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

546
00:35:05,757 --> 00:35:10,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have it to get pleasure from it and escape from the displeasure of it.

547
00:35:11,840 --> 00:35:12,481
[SPEAKER_01]: That's in itself.

548
00:35:13,597 --> 00:35:15,638
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not saying that, you're subjected to that.

549
00:35:16,618 --> 00:35:25,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the act of gratification, it's the act of seeking that pleasure, in order to escape pain, that reveals the danger, that's the danger in doing it, makes it dangerous.

550
00:35:26,161 --> 00:35:29,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means in itself, these things arising on their own, it's just how they are.

551
00:35:32,423 --> 00:35:37,945
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your way of involving yourself with them, that reveals the danger.

552
00:35:38,585 --> 00:35:39,606
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your way of...

553
00:35:39,846 --> 00:35:41,106
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger becomes apparent...

554
00:35:43,449 --> 00:35:43,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

555
00:35:44,470 --> 00:35:45,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Following the gratification.

556
00:35:45,952 --> 00:35:46,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

557
00:35:46,592 --> 00:35:47,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that dangerous?

558
00:36:16,980 --> 00:36:18,981
[SPEAKER_00]: How dare you?

559
00:36:25,926 --> 00:36:36,152
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being pressured by the central object that's in front of you, that you can have now, that's the reason state of affairs.

560
00:36:38,394 --> 00:36:40,955
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the arisen experience as a whole.

561
00:36:40,975 --> 00:36:44,215
[SPEAKER_01]: The unpleasant feeling of not having this, the mind grip wanting it.

562
00:36:44,676 --> 00:36:47,036
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing as a whole has arisen as such.

563
00:36:47,056 --> 00:36:47,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

564
00:36:48,997 --> 00:36:52,057
[SPEAKER_00]: You, having desire towards it, acting out of it.

565
00:36:52,117 --> 00:36:52,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

566
00:36:52,757 --> 00:36:58,159
[SPEAKER_00]: You're then responding to something which has arisen, which I have no... Yeah, but see, that's again how you're explaining it linearly.

567
00:36:58,239 --> 00:36:59,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

568
00:36:59,139 --> 00:37:02,980
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, can you act towards it?

569
00:37:03,858 --> 00:37:09,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Without implying that you want that same thing that you're acting towards to change.

570
00:37:10,938 --> 00:37:12,399
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, your experience as a whole.

571
00:37:13,039 --> 00:37:16,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be acting out of being pressured by the desire?

572
00:37:16,862 --> 00:37:22,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you be acting out of being pressured by the desire without implying, I want to change this?

573
00:37:22,265 --> 00:37:22,945
[SPEAKER_01]: I want this changed.

574
00:37:23,285 --> 00:37:26,067
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want this situation to be like that, I want it to be like that.

575
00:37:26,527 --> 00:37:38,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if there is a... like, if you don't practice restraint, if you're unrestrained householder layman, who lives, like, just follows his sensual pleasures, say, a new object of sensuality appears,

576
00:37:39,427 --> 00:37:40,668
[SPEAKER_01]: And he knows he'll have it.

577
00:37:41,088 --> 00:37:42,629
[SPEAKER_01]: He is going for it in a second.

578
00:37:44,670 --> 00:37:50,234
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience of his desire, that has been manifested when he became aware of the object, is unpleasant.

579
00:37:51,615 --> 00:37:53,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not pleasant enough.

580
00:37:53,616 --> 00:37:53,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

581
00:37:53,896 --> 00:37:54,857
[SPEAKER_00]: From his point of view.

582
00:37:55,157 --> 00:37:57,919
[SPEAKER_00]: This situation I'm in now, is not pleasant enough.

583
00:37:57,979 --> 00:38:00,420
[SPEAKER_00]: It could be more pleasant if I go... if I get that.

584
00:38:01,281 --> 00:38:01,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

585
00:38:01,681 --> 00:38:02,462
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's just...

586
00:38:02,482 --> 00:38:07,485
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, whichever way you take it, fundamentally, you're avoiding pain.

587
00:38:08,618 --> 00:38:10,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you want... Because you want something to change.

588
00:38:10,619 --> 00:38:11,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Less pain.

589
00:38:11,640 --> 00:38:15,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't... if you know you can't change it, doesn't mean you... you still want change.

590
00:38:16,042 --> 00:38:16,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

591
00:38:17,363 --> 00:38:17,963
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the risk.

592
00:38:20,105 --> 00:38:20,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting change.

593
00:38:22,706 --> 00:38:29,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And can you engage, can you give in, can you act out of your desire without implying you want change?

594
00:38:30,698 --> 00:38:30,818
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

595
00:38:30,838 --> 00:38:31,819
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what desire is.

596
00:38:32,079 --> 00:38:39,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Either change of this pleasure for more pleasure, or change of this pain for less pain, or change of this pleasure with... this pain for complete pleasure.

597
00:38:39,781 --> 00:38:40,422
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what change.

598
00:38:41,542 --> 00:38:45,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's the change that made you pressure... that pressured you in the first place.

599
00:38:46,544 --> 00:38:49,445
[SPEAKER_00]: The change pressured me in the first place.

600
00:38:49,485 --> 00:38:49,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

601
00:38:51,586 --> 00:38:57,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what else is arising of desire than change of circumstances that you were pressured by?

602
00:39:01,728 --> 00:39:03,169
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you mean something changed?

603
00:39:03,289 --> 00:39:05,411
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I mean, like, there was no sensual object here.

604
00:39:05,431 --> 00:39:05,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

605
00:39:05,791 --> 00:39:06,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And now there is.

606
00:39:06,512 --> 00:39:06,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

607
00:39:07,332 --> 00:39:07,933
[SPEAKER_00]: It changed.

608
00:39:07,993 --> 00:39:08,333
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

609
00:39:08,493 --> 00:39:09,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

610
00:39:09,134 --> 00:39:10,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something changed.

611
00:39:10,815 --> 00:39:10,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

612
00:39:11,315 --> 00:39:16,419
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of which, I now find that there's this possibility of pleasure.

613
00:39:16,819 --> 00:39:22,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Can that possibility of pleasure be acted out without approving and implicitly wanting more change?

614
00:39:22,984 --> 00:39:23,224
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

615
00:39:23,926 --> 00:39:26,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the change is the only reason you were pressured in the first place.

616
00:39:27,008 --> 00:39:27,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

617
00:39:27,528 --> 00:39:34,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, your only means of escaping change is to approve the change, which makes you more liable to pressure of change.

618
00:39:34,932 --> 00:39:35,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

619
00:39:36,013 --> 00:39:53,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for a leper with a skin disease, fire, cauterizing his wound, is the only means of escape that he knows, that it's making his wounds even worse, that he needs to cauterize even more, that will make the wounds even worse, that he needs to cauterize more.

620
00:39:54,350 --> 00:40:07,994
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the danger of gratification, of following the desire, is the implicit approval, implicit appropriation, implicit wanting of change.

621
00:40:08,814 --> 00:40:13,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's because of the change that you have this desire that bothers you in the first place.

622
00:40:14,896 --> 00:40:18,917
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you make yourself liable to the risk by doing it.

623
00:40:19,437 --> 00:40:21,277
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the things have changed.

624
00:40:21,397 --> 00:40:22,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Things change on their own.

625
00:40:22,698 --> 00:40:23,138
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

626
00:40:23,158 --> 00:40:23,758
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said,

627
00:40:24,482 --> 00:40:26,143
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you know you're pressured by feelings?

628
00:40:26,444 --> 00:40:27,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you give in.

629
00:40:28,526 --> 00:40:33,830
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't give in, if you stop giving in to the pressure of feelings, you can't say anymore, feelings pressure me.

630
00:40:33,850 --> 00:40:39,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the measure of how much you're pressured is the measure of how much you acted out of feelings.

631
00:40:39,976 --> 00:40:40,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Even mentally.

632
00:40:40,997 --> 00:40:43,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Even mentally, exactly.

633
00:40:43,819 --> 00:40:49,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can you say that the the danger is the change?

634
00:40:52,865 --> 00:40:56,048
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is change.

635
00:40:56,948 --> 00:41:00,451
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just see change in things, that's not the danger that you need.

636
00:41:00,711 --> 00:41:01,952
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't see it as dangerous.

637
00:41:02,772 --> 00:41:10,978
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see that you're giving in, your approval, you're welcoming, delighting in, being happy on account of the prospect of satisfying desire.

638
00:41:11,837 --> 00:41:15,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot be done without implying change.

639
00:41:15,260 --> 00:41:19,724
[SPEAKER_01]: So, cannot be done without making yourself subjected to the risk of change.

640
00:41:19,884 --> 00:41:20,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the greatest risk?

641
00:41:22,346 --> 00:41:24,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Life changes for death.

642
00:41:24,548 --> 00:41:25,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Gain changes for loss.

643
00:41:25,829 --> 00:41:28,672
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why is change dangerous?

644
00:41:28,992 --> 00:41:29,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

645
00:41:29,332 --> 00:41:31,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can ask that question now.

646
00:41:31,554 --> 00:41:36,859
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the gratification reveals the danger, because there is no gratification without implicit change.

647
00:41:37,059 --> 00:41:39,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, change is dangerous.

648
00:41:39,707 --> 00:41:40,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is change dangerous?

649
00:41:40,627 --> 00:41:41,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is change dangerous?

650
00:41:42,488 --> 00:41:47,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Because...

651
00:41:47,449 --> 00:41:51,309
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the idea...

652
00:41:51,329 --> 00:41:55,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because change is not this continuous... things continuously just change.

653
00:41:56,810 --> 00:41:58,871
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... No.

654
00:41:59,411 --> 00:41:59,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

655
00:41:59,751 --> 00:41:59,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

656
00:41:59,971 --> 00:42:01,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... Go on.

657
00:42:01,171 --> 00:42:09,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, it's something... Change is completely, like... comes from a completely conceivable direction.

658
00:42:10,297 --> 00:42:13,058
[SPEAKER_00]: I find that things have already changed.

659
00:42:13,879 --> 00:42:19,201
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't... it's something that's totally beyond my power, me.

660
00:42:19,301 --> 00:42:19,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Abrupt.

661
00:42:20,581 --> 00:42:20,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

662
00:42:21,442 --> 00:42:22,342
[SPEAKER_01]: It happens to you.

663
00:42:22,602 --> 00:42:22,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

664
00:42:23,743 --> 00:42:25,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as we describe in terms of feelings.

665
00:42:25,823 --> 00:42:32,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even people who know, people who are not relinquishing sensuality, practicing dhamma, they still know you can't control feelings.

666
00:42:32,266 --> 00:42:33,307
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subjected to them.

667
00:42:33,647 --> 00:42:33,947
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

668
00:42:34,733 --> 00:42:40,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so you can't look at something change, rather you just go, well, that's changed.

669
00:42:40,215 --> 00:42:47,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the change we talk about is not the change of my observation, which is fully on, like, what I want to do, or some, like, metaphysical theory.

670
00:42:48,278 --> 00:42:49,918
[SPEAKER_01]: The change is the change of how you feel.

671
00:42:50,819 --> 00:42:51,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not saying that.

672
00:42:52,759 --> 00:42:57,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, you can only understand gratification if you understand the pressure.

673
00:42:58,365 --> 00:43:01,627
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only understand the danger if you understood gratification.

674
00:43:02,107 --> 00:43:03,648
[SPEAKER_01]: That means understanding all the pressure.

675
00:43:04,008 --> 00:43:05,729
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the pressure other than feeling?

676
00:43:07,110 --> 00:43:15,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, if you still think, if a person still think on the level of, oh, this is one thing, one thing changes, second thing, second thing, that has nothing to do with what we are talking about.

677
00:43:15,195 --> 00:43:20,398
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just the domain of science and physics, which doesn't have the observer that is self-aware.

678
00:43:21,959 --> 00:43:24,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you account for feelings in physics and mathematics?

679
00:43:25,161 --> 00:43:26,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there you go.

680
00:43:27,022 --> 00:43:28,323
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot account for the dhamma.

681
00:43:29,583 --> 00:43:30,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Full stop.

682
00:43:33,005 --> 00:43:34,686
[SPEAKER_00]: You... yeah, so basically...

683
00:43:34,706 --> 00:43:37,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, change is change on the emotional level.

684
00:43:38,048 --> 00:43:41,830
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what Buddha said when he asked people, oh, so what?

685
00:43:41,850 --> 00:43:44,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Your pleasure... your... your... your self is... is... is your pleasure?

686
00:43:45,092 --> 00:43:45,552
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, yes.

687
00:43:45,812 --> 00:43:49,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so why don't you... why... why... why are you then feeling pain sometimes?

688
00:43:49,674 --> 00:43:50,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you sometimes feeling neutral?

689
00:43:51,835 --> 00:43:54,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if your feeling is yours, as in, if you would be in control,

690
00:43:55,162 --> 00:43:55,922
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't change.

691
00:43:55,982 --> 00:43:56,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

692
00:43:56,162 --> 00:43:57,723
[SPEAKER_01]: It would always be the way you want it to be.

693
00:43:59,344 --> 00:44:01,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are subject to change.

694
00:44:01,284 --> 00:44:01,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

695
00:44:02,985 --> 00:44:05,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, at the level of feeling.

696
00:44:05,346 --> 00:44:06,566
[SPEAKER_01]: The direction... Yeah.

697
00:44:07,587 --> 00:44:11,308
[SPEAKER_01]: The direction where change comes from, as you said, it's completely inconceivable to you.

698
00:44:11,528 --> 00:44:12,468
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's risky.

699
00:44:13,829 --> 00:44:22,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if something... if you have absolutely no idea how can this change, and whether it's going to change in agreeable way or non-agreeable way,

700
00:44:23,354 --> 00:44:25,295
[SPEAKER_01]: And what extent of the non-agreeable way?

701
00:44:25,775 --> 00:44:27,595
[SPEAKER_01]: And what harm of the non-agreeable way?

702
00:44:29,776 --> 00:44:36,258
[SPEAKER_01]: If all those things you cannot even account for in theory, would you regard that thing as non-risk-free?

703
00:44:36,778 --> 00:44:38,098
[SPEAKER_00]: No, at all.

704
00:44:38,139 --> 00:44:39,379
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be extremely risky.

705
00:44:39,399 --> 00:44:40,159
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the danger.

706
00:44:40,459 --> 00:44:41,219
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the danger.

707
00:44:41,680 --> 00:44:44,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, seeing change over there, you don't see the danger.

708
00:44:44,741 --> 00:44:49,102
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see the right order of things for danger to become apparent, as the Buddha said.

709
00:44:49,882 --> 00:44:52,883
[SPEAKER_01]: And the right order is, first, you need to understand the gratification of it.

710
00:44:54,235 --> 00:44:57,256
[SPEAKER_00]: And to understand the gratification of it, you have to, first of all, see...

711
00:44:57,276 --> 00:44:58,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Learn how to feel.

712
00:44:58,256 --> 00:44:59,776
[SPEAKER_01]: ...the fact that you've been pressured.

713
00:44:59,936 --> 00:45:00,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

714
00:45:00,676 --> 00:45:05,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Allow the feeling, the pressures you took with you, stop trying to get rid of it.

715
00:45:05,777 --> 00:45:15,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Through explaining it, through following a meditation technique, through trying to sort of deny it, to try to say, oh, well, look, this body is made of atoms and sensations, nothing is real.

716
00:45:15,659 --> 00:45:18,060
[SPEAKER_01]: All form of management of that which pressures you.

717
00:45:18,140 --> 00:45:19,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only reason you're doing it is because you're still pressured.

718
00:45:21,067 --> 00:45:24,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've been pressured, there's a feeling, there's gratification.

719
00:45:25,930 --> 00:45:29,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratification already starts at the prospect of changing the feeling that you don't want.

720
00:45:29,733 --> 00:45:30,153
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

721
00:45:30,453 --> 00:45:38,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Changing pleasure for more pleasure, changing pain for less pain, changing your thought for, basically, distraction, not being aware of it, because it's unpleasant when you're aware of it.

722
00:45:43,081 --> 00:45:50,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's inconceivable to give in to desire without wanting, implying that change.

723
00:45:51,006 --> 00:45:52,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's that change.

724
00:45:52,627 --> 00:45:55,049
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no saying that puts you in this position in the first place.

725
00:45:57,111 --> 00:46:07,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, you keep yourself... bound within that same cycle of... ...willingly bound in that absolutely, like, not even a cycle, like a... just a perpetual domain of perpetual risk.

726
00:46:08,020 --> 00:46:08,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

727
00:46:09,301 --> 00:46:09,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Chaos.

728
00:46:10,362 --> 00:46:10,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

729
00:46:14,045 --> 00:46:16,667
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's then, like, the easiest way to deal with it?

730
00:46:16,748 --> 00:46:17,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignore it.

731
00:46:17,969 --> 00:46:22,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, basically, I'm just... Yeah, that being prey to suffering.

732
00:46:23,132 --> 00:46:24,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the definition of it, yeah.

733
00:46:26,514 --> 00:46:29,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Being subject to suffering, being prey to suffering.

734
00:46:29,555 --> 00:46:33,397
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what being subject to suffering... That's what being subject to suffering means.

735
00:46:33,597 --> 00:46:33,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

736
00:46:34,698 --> 00:46:36,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, I don't necessarily need to be suffering now.

737
00:46:37,039 --> 00:46:38,140
[SPEAKER_01]: I am subject to suffering.

738
00:46:38,160 --> 00:46:38,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

739
00:46:39,572 --> 00:46:43,415
[SPEAKER_01]: In the nature, in my own nature, I can be affected by suffering.

740
00:46:43,795 --> 00:46:56,543
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only way to stop that is to see the danger involved in wanting things to change, to be otherwise than they are.

741
00:46:56,643 --> 00:46:56,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

742
00:46:57,824 --> 00:47:00,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you want things to change without craving?

743
00:47:01,767 --> 00:47:01,967
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

744
00:47:02,367 --> 00:47:03,108
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what craving is.

745
00:47:04,609 --> 00:47:06,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, craving is the root of suffering.

746
00:47:07,193 --> 00:47:08,293
[SPEAKER_01]: No craving, no suffering.

747
00:47:08,773 --> 00:47:11,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Even amidst the adverse circumstances that would otherwise make you suffer.

748
00:47:15,095 --> 00:47:16,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when the danger becomes apparent.

749
00:47:17,156 --> 00:47:18,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the danger of this, the danger of that.

750
00:47:19,216 --> 00:47:25,158
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger of me, if I want this, that's how I already make myself liable to that danger.

751
00:47:27,578 --> 00:47:34,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, restraining yourself in regard to sensuality prevents some danger, but if you still want it in your mind, to that extent you're still liable to suffer.

752
00:47:37,417 --> 00:47:40,159
[SPEAKER_01]: When things change the way you don't want them to.

753
00:47:42,041 --> 00:47:44,463
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, when your feelings change the way you don't want them to.

754
00:47:45,924 --> 00:47:59,015
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize that even your sense of control towards perceptions, intentions and so on, it's completely secondary to the fundamental liability to the feeling that you have no say in.

755
00:47:59,315 --> 00:47:59,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

756
00:47:59,996 --> 00:48:01,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, then, your control is not really in your...

757
00:48:01,677 --> 00:48:02,458
[SPEAKER_01]: This is subject to change.

758
00:48:02,658 --> 00:48:02,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

759
00:48:03,099 --> 00:48:04,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Your control, then, is not really your control.

760
00:48:06,185 --> 00:48:08,086
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the inherent nature of feelings.

761
00:48:08,146 --> 00:48:09,087
[SPEAKER_00]: They are subject to change.

762
00:48:09,107 --> 00:48:10,428
[SPEAKER_00]: They are of the nature to change.

763
00:48:10,448 --> 00:48:11,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

764
00:48:11,348 --> 00:48:12,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature to be otherwise.

765
00:48:12,409 --> 00:48:13,570
[SPEAKER_01]: The parināma dhamma.

766
00:48:15,631 --> 00:48:18,873
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that's not what the danger is.

767
00:48:19,594 --> 00:48:22,175
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger isn't the fact that...

768
00:48:22,195 --> 00:48:23,756
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is...

769
00:48:23,776 --> 00:48:26,258
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is the...

770
00:48:28,075 --> 00:48:29,136
[SPEAKER_00]: He's wanting them to change.

771
00:48:29,976 --> 00:48:31,557
[SPEAKER_00]: He's approving.

772
00:48:31,998 --> 00:48:36,681
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger is approving of change, through implicitly approving of change, through giving it to a desire.

773
00:48:37,802 --> 00:48:39,763
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you know change is a problem because it bothers you.

774
00:48:39,783 --> 00:48:40,644
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of it.

775
00:48:41,544 --> 00:48:44,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the only means of getting rid of it is approving the means of change.

776
00:48:46,167 --> 00:48:49,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence the utter discrepancy and contradiction on a very existential level.

777
00:48:49,490 --> 00:48:51,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence the suffering, the liability to it.

778
00:48:52,973 --> 00:48:53,513
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

779
00:48:53,773 --> 00:48:55,494
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not dangerous that things change.

780
00:48:55,714 --> 00:48:55,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

781
00:48:56,134 --> 00:48:59,295
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dangerous you to use means of change to change things.

782
00:48:59,415 --> 00:48:59,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

783
00:49:01,815 --> 00:49:02,815
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the danger is.

784
00:49:02,935 --> 00:49:05,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you insert yourself and that's why you become subject to these things.

785
00:49:06,116 --> 00:49:06,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to change.

786
00:49:06,916 --> 00:49:07,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Change.

787
00:49:08,757 --> 00:49:08,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

788
00:49:09,897 --> 00:49:10,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't want change.

789
00:49:11,558 --> 00:49:12,558
[SPEAKER_01]: And thus, I'll change it.

790
00:49:12,578 --> 00:49:12,978
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll change it.

791
00:49:13,758 --> 00:49:14,038
Yeah.

792
00:49:15,919 --> 00:49:16,279
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

793
00:49:16,539 --> 00:49:20,760
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the inherent condensed principle of sensuality, basically.

794
00:49:21,180 --> 00:49:21,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And your will.

795
00:49:22,648 --> 00:49:23,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

796
00:49:24,489 --> 00:49:26,990
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want this change, so I change it.

797
00:49:27,291 --> 00:49:27,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

798
00:49:28,771 --> 00:49:30,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I don't know anything other than change.

799
00:49:31,053 --> 00:49:33,554
[SPEAKER_01]: So the... this change is not the risk.

800
00:49:34,114 --> 00:49:36,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Outcome of me changing it is not the risk.

801
00:49:37,396 --> 00:49:38,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it's all secondary to the risk.

802
00:49:38,777 --> 00:49:43,620
[SPEAKER_01]: The risk is the fact that I still approve of that option, so to speak.

803
00:49:43,920 --> 00:49:44,940
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the danger is.

804
00:49:45,041 --> 00:49:45,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

805
00:49:45,641 --> 00:49:46,821
